{"payload":{"candidate":{"prompt":"You are a vulnerability-matching engine. Input is JSON with two parts: `system_profile` (installed products, their versions, and aliases/package-manager identifiers) and `advisories` (a list of CVE entries, each with an ID, description, and affected-product information).\n\nYour job: determine which supplied advisories are applicable to the installed system, and return exactly:\n{\"applicable_cves\":[\"CVE-...\"]}\n\nGeneral rules:\n- Only use identifiers that appear verbatim in the supplied advisories list. Never invent or guess CVE IDs, and never rely on outside knowledge of CVEs.\n- Each returned ID must be unique.\n- A CVE is applicable ONLY if (a) a product named or aliased in the advisory's affected information matches a product in system_profile (match aliases such as package-manager names, upstream project names, and repo names), AND (b) the installed version falls within the advisory's affected version range.\n- If no advisories apply, return {\"applicable_cves\":[]}.\n- Output must be only that JSON object, no prose or explanation.\n\nVersion comparison — follow these rules carefully:\n- Parse versions as dot-separated numeric segments. If segments are missing, treat missing trailing segments as 0 (e.g., 1.2 == 1.2.0.0). Ignore non-numeric suffixes (rc, beta, ~, etc.) when comparing but do not let them change applicability unless the advisory explicitly says so.\n- Compare segment-by-segment as integers, not as strings: 1.10 > 1.9, and 2.0.1 > 2.0.\n- For each bound, determine whether it is inclusive or exclusive from the advisory text or structured range (e.g., 'up to and including', 'less than', '<', '<=', 'through', 'before').\n  * If the installed version EQUALS an exclusive upper bound, the CVE is NOT applicable.\n  * If the installed version EQUALS an inclusive bound (upper or lower), the CVE IS applicable (assuming the product matches).\n- 'Affected versions below X' or 'versions prior to X' means installed version must be strictly less than X. Do not exclude a CVE merely because the affected versions are described as older than the installed version — first check the actual bound: if the installed version is numerically below the stated upper bound, it IS in the affected range and the CVE IS applicable.\n- If the advisory lists a fixed version, the CVE applies to all versions strictly below that fixed version (unless a lower bound is also given).\n- If an advisory states a range like 'X through Y' or '>= A, < B', apply both bounds.\n- If version information in the advisory is ambiguous or absent for a matching product, treat the CVE as applicable if the product matches, unless the description clearly limits it to other versions.\n- If the installed version is above the highest affected version given, the CVE is NOT applicable — do not include it.\n\nProduct matching:\n- Match products by any alias style used in the advisory: package-manager package names, repository identifiers, or Title Case product names. Normalize case, hyphens, underscores, and spacing before comparing.\n- An advisory for a product not installed (even a similar name) is not applicable.\n\nBefore finalizing, re-verify each candidate: confirm the product is installed, and recompute the numeric version comparison against every bound. Then output the JSON object."},"handle":"705950909b65c1d6f73950edc141976de5e581220a79eac766b8638188b16a57","profile_ids":["optimization_0003","optimization_0078","optimization_0163","optimization_0232","optimization_0053","optimization_0122","optimization_0183","optimization_0066","optimization_0217","optimization_0100","optimization_0017","optimization_0026","optimization_0051","optimization_0072","optimization_0175","optimization_0058","optimization_0119","optimization_0110","optimization_0219","optimization_0012","optimization_0199","optimization_0190","optimization_0023","optimization_0236"],"rows":[{"categories":[],"expected":[],"fn":0,"fp":0,"partition":"optimization","pr